# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes homogeneous function parameter packs for C++29. Their parameters share one declared element type and use ordinary parameter initialization. An invented constant template parameter represents the pack length. Its type is <code>std::size_t</code>, consistent with <code>sizeof...</code>. The count can be deduced or supplied explicitly and can determine a result type. One declaration can replace families of fixed-arity overloads while retaining ordinary conversions to the declared type.

C++26 clause references use N5050. [[N5050]]

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" id=pack-matrix>
<caption>Parameter-pack declarations in C++26. Each declaration is independent. The lower-right declaration is ill-formed today and would be permitted by this proposal.</caption>
<colgroup><col style="width: 24%"><col style="width: 38%"><col style="width: 38%"></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element types</th><th scope=col>Template parameter pack</th><th scope=col>Function parameter pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Heterogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Homogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Proposed (ill-formed in C++26): vs is a pack of T parameters.
template <class T>
void foo(T... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

The matrix shows the gap: C++26 permits three of the four forms. The other forms suggest that both template and function parameter packs can have either homogeneous or heterogeneous elements, so the missing lower-right form can be confusing. In current C++, <code>T... vs</code> declares a function parameter pack whose pattern expands an existing type parameter pack. In the lower-right declaration, <code>T</code> is one type parameter rather than a pack to expand. That form is ill-formed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general#2">[dcl.spec.auto.general]/2</a> <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/24</a> The proposal instead uses that single <code>T</code> as the type of each parameter while declaring <code>vs</code> as a function parameter pack.

Each element of the proposed function pack would be an ordinary function parameter initialized at the call. When its type is fixed or supplied explicitly, arguments with different source types can undergo ordinary conversions to that type. If the type is deduced, the arguments must satisfy the applicable deduction rules. The pack's length would be deduced at compile time and could determine a result type, while its values could be computed at runtime. The homogeneous template pack instead contains constant template arguments. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a>

Abseil's <a href="#example-count"><code>Partial</code></a> motivates conversion diagnostics at the call and a count-dependent result type. CasADi's <a href="#example-casadi">matrix concatenation</a> demonstrates the difference in conversion ranking between fixed parameters and a constrained deduced pack in constructed calls. Current C++ alternatives include generated fixed-arity overloads for Abseil and explicit-destination grouped calls or fixed-arity wrappers for CasADi.

The proposal permits one homogeneous pack at the end of a function's parameter list. Its declaration makes the function an abbreviated function template. Each specialization has an ordinary function type with a fixed number of parameters. The count participates in specialization identity through its template argument. Existing pack expansions retain their meanings.

Declaration-site length syntax and multiple homogeneous packs are out of scope. Explicit count template arguments are supported. The named form <code>int... values</code> is unambiguous and can proceed independently of the <a href="#compatibility">removal and reclamation decision</a>. Unnamed homogeneous packs require reclaiming <code>int...</code>, which is deprecated but still means C varargs in C++26. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a> Both decisions remain in P1219R3, with <a href="#proposed-polls">separate proposed EWG polls</a>. A separate removal paper remains a fallback if EWG feedback warrants it.

# Motivating use cases # {#examples}

The examples concern two requirements that constraints on deduced argument types do not preserve: conversion diagnostics at the call and conversion ranking between overloaded interfaces. Abseil also needs the argument count in its result type. Current and proposed definitions are independent alternatives.

## Combine call-site diagnostics with count-dependent results ## {#example-count}

Abseil's internal <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> describes one array per element type in a single allocation. <code>Partial</code> takes element counts for the first arrays and records the number of arguments in its result type, <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>. The size values may be computed at runtime. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

For runtime <code>size_t</code> values <code>int_count</code> and <code>double_count</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
using ArrayLayout =
    absl::container_internal::Layout<int, double, char>;

auto ints_known = ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count);
auto ints_and_doubles_known =
    ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count, double_count);
</xmp>

The first call returns <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;1&gt;</code> and permits querying the <code>int</code> and <code>double</code> array offsets. The second returns <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code> and also permits querying the <code>char</code> array's offset:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto double_offset = ints_known.Offset<double>();
auto char_offset = ints_and_doubles_known.Offset<char>();
</xmp>

Calling <code>ints_known.Offset&lt;char&gt;()</code> is rejected at compile time because the preceding <code>double</code> array's size was not supplied. The argument count selects the result type and determines which offsets can be calculated. The runtime size values determine the offset values. A replacement for <code>Partial</code> needs to retain this contract. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

The shipped declaration deduces every argument type to obtain the count, then casts each argument to <code>size_t</code>. A homogeneous pack would deduce only the count and initialize <code>size_t</code> parameters at the call:

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Abseil's <code>Partial</code> and a proposed homogeneous declaration. Both retain the argument-count check.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Abseil at d522178</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Sizes>
static constexpr PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>
Partial(Sizes&&... sizes) {
  static_assert(sizeof...(Sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>(
      static_cast<size_t>(
          std::forward<Sizes>(sizes))...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
static constexpr auto
Partial(size_t... sizes)
    -> PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)> {
  static_assert(sizeof...(sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)>(
      sizes...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>

For an <code>int signed_count</code>, compare <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(3)</code> with <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(signed_count)</code>. Abseil's authors want conversion warnings for the signed variable but not the positive literal. Their <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L760-L780">comment</a> explains that explicit casts suppress warnings for both arguments. Implicit conversions inside the forwarding function's body can instead warn for both.

A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;size_t&gt;</code> constraint does not move those body conversions to the call. Fixed <code>size_t</code> parameters move the conversions to the call so the compiler sees the original argument expression (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/concept.convertible#1">[concept.convertible]/1</a> and <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6</a>).

Warnings depend on the implementation and enabled options. Negative integer arguments remain well-formed and convert to unsigned values. Ordinary parameter initialization also rejects explicit-only conversions accepted by the shipped <code>static_cast</code>, such as an explicit <code>operator size_t()</code> (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/conv.integral#3">[conv.integral]/3</a>, <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.conv#1">[over.match.conv]/1</a>, and <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.static.cast#5">[expr.static.cast]/5</a>).

Current C++ can provide the same parameter conversions and count-dependent result types through a bounded overload set. For plain <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> with no statically supplied sizes, generate one overload for each count from zero through <code>sizeof...(Ts)</code>. The header already expands an existing index pack through its <code>IntToSize</code> alias to give the <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L399-L400">constructor</a> <code>size_t</code> parameters. In this fragment, <code>LayoutType&lt;N, Ts...&gt;</code> stands for Abseil's <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <size_t>
using IntToSize = size_t;

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload;

template <size_t... Is, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload<std::index_sequence<Is...>, Ts...> {
  static constexpr LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>
  Partial(IntToSize<Is>... sizes) {
    return LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>(sizes...);
  }
};

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads;

template <size_t... Ns, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads<std::index_sequence<Ns...>, Ts...>
    : PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>... {
  using PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>::Partial...;
};
</xmp>

<code>Layout</code> then inherits <code>PartialOverloads&lt;std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(Ts) + 1&gt;, Ts...&gt;</code>. For <code>ArrayLayout</code>, <code>Ns...</code> enumerates the counts 0, 1, 2, and 3. Each count generates an index sequence <code>Is...</code> of that length. Expanding <code>IntToSize&lt;Is&gt;... sizes</code> gives an overload with one <code>size_t</code> parameter per index. The using-declaration combines the overloads and the call selects one with a fixed parameter count.

The homogeneous pack expresses that interface in one function declaration, without generating and combining overloads.

## Preserve conversion ranking without fixed-arity wrappers ## {#example-casadi}

CasADi builds symbolic matrix expressions. Its <code>horzcat</code>, <code>vertcat</code>, and <code>diagcat</code> functions concatenate matrices horizontally, vertically, and diagonally. At commit d30fbc4, each operation has positional overloads for two through six arguments, giving fifteen wrappers. Each parameter is a <code>const MatType&amp;</code>, with <code>MatType</code> fixed by the enclosing <code>SparsityInterface</code> specialization. [[CASADI-CONCAT]] Library code uses <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/integration_tools.cpp#L930-L936"><code>vertcat(dx_cat, 1)</code></a>, relying on conversion of the scalar to the selected matrix type.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>CasADi's vertical concatenation wrappers, with two arities shown, and a proposed replacement for the family. Both initialize references to a fixed matrix type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>CasADi at d30fbc4</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y});
}

inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y,
    const MatType& z) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y, z});
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& first,
    const MatType& second,
    const MatType&... rest) {
  return MatType::vertcat(
      std::vector<MatType>{first, second, rest...});
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>

The ordinary parameters retain the minimum of two operands. The trailing pack removes the six-argument ceiling. The same change applies to horizontal and diagonal concatenation.

A constrained deduced pack also removes the overload family. Consider this alternative inside the same class:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Args>
  requires (sizeof...(Args) >= 2 &&
            (std::is_convertible_v<
                const Args&, const MatType&> && ...))
friend MatType vertcat(const Args&... args) {
  return MatType::vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{args...});
}
</xmp>

This alternative changes overload resolution. CasADi's <code>SX</code> is a matrix of symbolic expressions and <code>IM</code> is an integer matrix. For a mutable <code>SX symbolic</code> and <code>IM indices</code>, <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L285-L288">indexing</a> produces a <code>SubIndex&lt;SX, IM&gt;</code> proxy derived from <code>SX</code>. [[CASADI-PROXY]] The following calls are constructed checks of that interface:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
vertcat(symbolic(indices), 1);
vertcat(1, symbolic(indices));
</xmp>

Argument-dependent lookup finds concatenation functions associated with both <code>SX</code> and the proxy's <code>IM</code> template argument. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/basic.lookup.argdep#3">[basic.lookup.argdep]/3</a> The existing fixed parameters select the <code>SX</code> overload in both calls: binding the proxy to its base class is better than converting it to <code>IM</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.rank#2.1">[over.ics.rank]/2.1</a> With the deduced pack, both candidates instead bind the deduced argument types directly and satisfy the conversion constraints. Neither candidate is better, making both calls ambiguous. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> The homogeneous declaration retains the fixed destination types used to rank these conversions.

Keeping only the first parameter as <code>const MatType&amp;</code> leaves the second call ambiguous. With two fixed parameters, <code>vertcat(1, 1, symbolic(indices))</code> is still ambiguous because the distinguishing operand is in the deduced tail.

Competing <code>std::initializer_list&lt;SX&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;IM&gt;</code> overloads also make the grouped form ambiguous. List conversion ranking uses the worst element conversion. The scalar requires a user-defined conversion for either destination so the list ranking loses the distinction supplied by the indexed operand (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.list#5">[over.ics.list]/5</a>).

Current C++ already supports an arbitrary operand count through an explicit destination, such as <code>SX::vertcat({symbolic(indices), 1})</code>. Keeping or generating fixed-arity wrappers also preserves the positional calls. A homogeneous pack expresses their parameter conversions directly, with one declaration per operation and no fixed upper bound on the operand count.

# Motivation and scope # {#motivation}

Constrained packs, initializer lists, vectors, spans, arrays, ranges, and generated overload sets offer different combinations of ordinary conversion diagnostics, count-dependent result types, and ordinary conversion ranking.

## Requests for fixed parameter types ## {#expressivity}

<p>R2 cited eight Stack Overflow questions asking how to write a function with a homogeneous parameter pack. [[P1219R2]]</p>

<p>In May 2022, a programmer asked for a fixed parameter type with ordinary implicit conversions and explained why constraints on deduced types using <code>std::same_as</code> or <code>std::convertible_to</code> do not provide that behavior. [[SO-9]]
In January 2026, another asked how to declare a function taking an even number of <code>int</code> arguments and why templates were needed for concrete parameter types. [[SO-10]]</p>

## Initializer lists and vectors ## {#initializer-lists}

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Alternative homogeneous interfaces. The vector column concerns element types other than <code>bool</code>. Each interface can use a fixed element type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Property</th><th scope=col>Homogeneous pack</th><th scope=col><code>std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;</code></th><th scope=col><code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope=row>Elements and mutation</th><td>Ordinary value or reference parameters, with their declared cv-qualification</td><td>Access to const backing-array elements</td><td>Mutable owned elements through a non-const vector</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Ownership and lifetime</th><td>Value parameters own their values. Reference parameters borrow under ordinary lifetime rules</td><td>A view. Copying the list does not copy elements or extend the backing array's lifetime</td><td>Owns its storage and elements. A reference parameter borrows an existing vector</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Contiguous storage</th><td>No array or contiguity guarantee</td><td>Contiguous backing array</td><td>Contiguous storage</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Count and iteration</th><td>Count is a template argument. Use pack expansion, folds, or pack indexing</td><td>Use <code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration. Parameter value does not select a specialization</td><td>Use <code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration. Parameter value does not select a specialization</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Runtime-sized input</th><td>No direct expansion of a runtime-sized range into arguments</td><td>No constructor from an arbitrary runtime range</td><td>Accepts runtime-sized input and can grow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Storage and element work</th><td>No container allocation required. Each parameter undergoes ordinary initialization</td><td>Backing-array initialization. Copying the view does not copy its elements</td><td>Constructing storage can allocate and construct elements. Passing an existing vector by reference does neither</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Call syntax</th><td><code>f(a, b, c)</code></td><td><code>f({a, b, c})</code></td><td><code>f(existing)</code> or construction of a vector, for example <code>f({a, b, c})</code></td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Function declarations</th><td>A template with one trailing homogeneous pack</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

Both containers can have a size computable during constant evaluation. An ordinary function parameter's value still cannot supply a count-dependent return type or select a function specialization. The proposed count is a template argument independently of the runtime values, as required by Abseil's <code>PartialType</code> in [[#example-count]]. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/support.initlist">[support.initlist]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#10">[temp.variadic]/10</a>

Competing initializer-list overloads rank conversions differently from separate parameters. In CasADi's case, grouping the operands loses the distinction between the matrix conversions ([[#example-casadi]]). Constructor overload selection has a further difference, described in [[#constructors]].

A vector is preferable when the input already lives in a vector, its length is known only at runtime, or the operation needs an owned sequence, ordinary iteration, or growth. Taking <code>const std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;</code> avoids copying an existing vector. Taking <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;</code> permits mutating that vector. Passing a vector by value copies an lvalue vector when copying is supported. Passing an rvalue can instead transfer its storage through the ordinary move constructor. Passing a vector does not require constructing a temporary vector at every call. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/vector.overview">[vector.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/container.reqmts">[container.reqmts]</a>

Constructing a vector from an initializer list is a separate case. The list exposes const elements and the vector's initializer-list constructor constructs its own elements from const T references. That constructor therefore cannot generally construct <code>std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> from a braced list of owning pointers. Vectors themselves support move-only elements through suitable insertion or range construction. Homogeneous value parameters can receive move-only arguments through ordinary move initialization and reference parameters can borrow them. An initializer list can itself contain move-only objects, but its const access prevents ordinary moves out of those elements. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/sequence.reqmts">[sequence.reqmts]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/initializer.list.syn">[initializer.list.syn]</a>

A list of <code>std::reference_wrapper&lt;T&gt;</code> can still permit mutation of caller-owned objects. An initializer list also permits ownership transfer through user-defined wrappers with <code>mutable</code> members. Copies of the list share those members so consuming through one copy leaves a moved-from state visible through the others. These techniques preserve the list's const backing array and do not make its count a template argument. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.stc#9">[dcl.stc]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list#5">[dcl.init.list]/5</a>

Homogeneous packs preserve positional calls, ordinary per-parameter initialization, and conversion ranking ([[#example-casadi]]). They do not offer a general performance advantage over containers. Their parameter initialization can copy or move objects and their bodies can allocate. A vector can reuse storage. Separate count specializations can increase instantiation work and code size, although implementations can share generated code. Those costs need measurement for the intended use.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision.</strong> Confirm that Abseil's <code>Layout::Partial</code> is the maintained interface to keep in [[#example-count]].</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: research. Jan: review.</strong> Compare typed runtime variadics and checked C varargs, including D's variadics and GCC's argument-count builtin. Distinguish count from type checks and account for default promotions. <a href="https://dlang.org/spec/function.html#typesafe_variadic_functions">D typesafe variadics</a> <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Constructing-Calls.html#index-_005f_005fbuiltin_005fva_005farg_005fpack_005flen">GCC <code>__builtin_va_arg_pack_len</code></a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#12">[expr.call]/12</a></p>
</aside>

## Spans, arrays, and constrained ranges ## {#range-alternatives}

Spans and array references suit callers with existing contiguous storage. A constrained range suits ordinary iteration over the caller's sequence. Static-extent forms can also provide a specialization-dependent count. Homogeneous packs retain separate parameters and their individual initialization and overload ranking without requiring a common buffer. Choosing between them depends on those interface requirements, not a general performance advantage.

A <code>std::span&lt;T&gt;</code> borrows a contiguous sequence and supports ordinary iteration over runtime-sized input without allocating storage or copying the elements. It permits mutation when <code>T</code> permits it. Making the span object const does not make its elements const. <code>std::span&lt;const T&gt;</code> instead provides read-only element access. A span does not own its elements or extend their lifetime. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.elem">[span.elem]</a>

A dynamic-extent span does not encode its actual length in its type. A <code>std::span&lt;T, N&gt;</code> does. A template can deduce <code>N</code> and use it in a return type or constraint. Constructing a static span from runtime-sized input still requires its length to equal <code>N</code>. The runtime length does not deduce <code>N</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#18">[span.cons]/18</a> A <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code> or a reference to <code>T&#91;N]</code> can supply the same compile-time count. These alternatives can supply Abseil's count-dependent <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code> today. This current-language example makes the deduced extent part of each result type: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t N>
auto span_count(std::span<const std::size_t, N>) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

template <std::size_t N>
auto array_count(const std::size_t (&)[N]) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

const std::size_t sizes[]{3, 5};
auto from_span = span_count(std::span{sizes});
auto from_array = array_count(sizes);
auto from_braces = array_count({3, 5});
</xmp>

All three results have type <code>std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t, 2&gt;</code>. The grouped call <code>array_count({3, 5})</code> deduces the bound from the braced list and initializes a temporary array. An analogous array-reference <code>Partial({int_count, double_count})</code> could return <code>PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code> without named storage or an explicit extent. Its list-initialization rejects narrowing that ordinary <code>size_t</code> parameter initialization can permit, changing Abseil's signed-variable conversion case ([[#example-count]]). Binding an existing array to the reference instead copies no elements. A mutable array reference can modify the original elements. A reference parameter does not extend an existing array's lifetime. Static spans and <code>std::array</code> support extent zero, while a built-in array bound must be positive. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array.zero">[array.zero]</a>

Constructing a span from an existing array or range requires compatible element types. Its element-type constraints permit qualification conversions, not numeric or user-defined conversion of each stored element. An <code>int</code> array therefore cannot initialize a <code>std::span&lt;const std::size_t&gt;</code> merely because individual integers convert to <code>size_t</code>. A caller can choose the destination explicitly by first constructing a typed array or vector. A fixed const-element span parameter can also accept a double-braced argument such as <code>&#123;&#123;1, 2}}</code>, which initializes a temporary array with numeric or user-defined element conversions. List-initialization rejects narrowing and the temporary array lasts until the end of the full-expression containing the call. These grouped forms preserve neither the original positional syntax nor CasADi's per-parameter conversion ranking ([[#example-casadi]]). <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#13">[span.cons]/13</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/span.cons#16">[span.cons]/16</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a>

A <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code> owns its fixed-size contiguous sequence and permits mutation through a non-const object. Passing an existing array by reference avoids element copies. Passing it by value can copy or move its elements, while direct initialization from a prvalue need not add a copy or move. Explicitly choosing <code>T</code> supplies destinations for list-initialized elements, with narrowing checks. Class template argument deduction instead requires the deduced element types to be the same. An array interface can therefore retain the count and fixed element type, but callers still provide a grouped object. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/array.cons">[array.cons]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init#general">[dcl.init.general]</a>

A constrained <code>std::ranges::input_range</code> interface accepts input through iteration, including runtime-sized and non-contiguous sequences. That concept alone guarantees neither contiguity, a size operation, mutation, ownership, nor a static extent. A forwarding-reference parameter can borrow an existing range without copying its elements. Deducing the concrete range type can preserve a static extent when the supplied type carries one, such as a static span or array. Requiring element convertibility can constrain an implementation's conversions, but conversions performed during iteration remain in the body rather than at the original positional call ([[#example-count]]). <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/range.refinements">[range.refinements]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/range.sized">[range.sized]</a>

# Design decisions # {#design-decisions}

## Definitions ## {#definitions}

A <em>homogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>homogeneous pack</em>) contains values of one type. A <em>heterogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>heterogeneous pack</em>) permits values of different types. Both terms apply to function parameter packs and template parameter packs. A homogeneous function parameter pack introduces an invented constant template parameter for its length.

A function parameter pack's <em>parameter pattern</em> is its parameter declaration with the declarator ellipsis removed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.1">[temp.variadic]/6.1</a> For an <code>auto...</code> parameter, ordinary placeholder processing first invents a type parameter pack. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a> After placeholder processing, a declaration is homogeneous only if its pattern contains no unexpanded pack. Classifying the pattern before substitution keeps an existing pack expansion ordinary even when all resulting parameter types are the same.

<p><strong>Which of these still expand an existing pack and which get a new homogeneous pack count?</strong></p>

This is after placeholder processing but before substituting arguments from an enclosing template. Each row is independent. Here, <code>T</code> is one type, <code>Ts</code> is a type pack, <code>Templates</code> is a compatible template template parameter pack, <code>Is</code> is an index pack, and <code>I</code> is one constant index. <code>Box</code> takes a type pack and <code>C</code> is a unary type concept.

<code>xs</code> can be a constant template parameter pack, function parameter pack, init-capture pack, or structured binding pack. The same classification rule applies to all four.

Each homogeneous case gets its own count. The types, indices, folds, and array bounds still have to be valid. A count of zero does not skip substitution into the element type. The nested requirement in the last row determines the value of a requires-expression, whose type is <code>bool</code>. It does not constrain the surrounding function.

An occurrence is unexpanded in the parameter pattern unless it belongs to the pattern of a nested pack expansion. This is determined per occurrence. Indexing consumes the indexed pack name but leaves any unexpanded pack in the index for an enclosing expansion. Neither <code>decltype</code> nor ordinary <code>sizeof</code> consumes a pack. The existing expansion boundaries stay as they are in <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6">[temp.variadic]/6</a>, along with the constraint rules in <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.fold">[temp.constr.fold]</a>.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Parameter-pack classification under this proposal.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Parameter declaration</th><th scope=col>What's still unexpanded</th><th scope=col>Meaning under the proposal</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>int</code> pack</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>T... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>T</code> pack</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto... values</code></td><td>Invented type pack</td><td>Ordinary expansion</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts... values</code></td><td><code>Ts</code></td><td>Expands <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Templates&lt;T&gt;... values</code></td><td><code>Templates</code></td><td>Expands <code>Templates</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs)... values</code></td><td><code>xs</code></td><td>Expands <code>xs</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(sizeof(xs))... values</code></td><td><code>xs</code></td><td>Still expands <code>xs</code>. Getting the same type each time does not change that</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Box&lt;Ts...&gt;... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the resulting <code>Box</code> type. The inner expansion already uses <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts...&#91;I]... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the selected type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts...[Is]... values</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code>. Indexing uses <code>Ts</code> but leaves <code>Is</code> for the parameter expansion</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...&#91;I])... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack using the selected element's <code>decltype</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...[Is])... values</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code>. Indexing uses <code>xs</code> but leaves <code>Is</code> for the parameter expansion</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...&#91;I] + xs)... values</code></td><td>The second <code>xs</code></td><td>Expands that second <code>xs</code>. Indexing only consumes the first occurrence</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype((xs + ...))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the fold's result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(sizeof...(xs))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the <code>sizeof...</code> result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int... values[Is]</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Still expands <code>Is</code>, even though array parameter adjustment gives <code>int*</code> each time</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(requires { requires (C&lt;Ts&gt; &amp;&amp; ...); })... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>bool</code> pack. The fold-expanded constraint in the nested requirement already consumes <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

## Pack declarations ## {#implicit-template}

The same declarator syntax can express either an ordinary expansion or a new homogeneous pack. The pattern need not mention a template parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// heterogeneous function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
template <class T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
void f(int... v);
</xmp>

A parameter pack declared with <code>auto... v</code> already permits different deduced argument types in C++26. The fixed-type form <code>int... v</code> is currently ill-formed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a>

A homogeneous function parameter pack makes the declaration an abbreviated function template. Its signature establishes that status before the body is parsed. Making the count a template argument gives calls, address-taking, and explicit instantiations one way to identify the same specialization. The parameter has no source name. Its argument can be deduced or supplied explicitly and <code>sizeof...(values)</code> exposes its value for a named pack.

The invented count parameter has type <code>std::size_t</code>, matching the result type of <code>sizeof...</code>. Normative wording identifies this as the type of a <code>sizeof</code> expression, without requiring a declaration of <code>std::size_t</code> or inclusion of a header. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.sizeof#5">[expr.sizeof]/5</a>

[[P1061R10]] section 3.3 removed support for structured binding packs outside templates after objections to R9's implicit template region. That region covered the rest of an ordinary function after a structured binding declaration. Here the signature declares the whole function a template. This distinction does not establish committee agreement on the proposal.

In a function call, the pack's size is deduced from the arguments. This revision requires a homogeneous pack to be the only function parameter pack and to appear last, with no following C ellipsis. Non-pack parameters may precede it, as in [[#example-casadi]]. This lets the remaining call arguments determine one count without rules for dividing them among packs or a C ellipsis. Once its parameters are formed, the pack behaves like any other function parameter pack.

The following declarations violate those placement rules:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void misplaced(int... values, int last);
void two_packs(int... left, double... right);
void trailing_varargs(int... values, ...);
</xmp>

A homogeneous pack declares parameters of the function itself or a lambda's call operator. The declarations <code>using Callback = void(int... values);</code> and <code>void register_callback(void (*callback)(int... values));</code> remain ill-formed. These forms do not declare the function template that would own the count. Supporting them would require separate rules for introducing and deducing the count. Ordinary pack expansions in aliases and nested function types are unchanged.

[[P2347R2]] proposes deducing the length of a single non-trailing function parameter pack from the number of arguments and required trailing parameters. Extending homogeneous packs to non-trailing positions is outside this revision's scope.

### Abbreviated syntax ### {#abbreviated-syntax}

Since C++20, a function template that needs no template parameter names can use abbreviated syntax, with placeholders such as <code>auto</code> in its parameter list. [[P1141R2]] <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a>

Even when the element type is fixed, the template has the invented count parameter. A declaration that needs no explicit template parameters omits the <code nohighlight>template-head</code>. An empty <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> retains its ordinary role as an explicit-specialization introducer and cannot introduce a new homogeneous pack.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void named_f(int... values);
auto* named_pf = &named_f<3>;

template <class T>
void named_g(T... values);
auto* named_pg = &named_g<int, 3>;
</xmp>

Both pointer variables have type <code>void (*)(int, int, int)</code>. These named declarations and explicit count arguments do not depend on reclaiming the comma-less spelling. With reclamation, the unnamed equivalents are also permitted:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void f(int...);
auto* pf = &f<3>;

template <class T>
void g(T...);
auto* pg = &g<int, 3>;
</xmp>

The invented count parameter follows all explicitly declared template parameters and all invented type parameters for <code>auto</code> parameters. Appending the count preserves the positions and relative order of those parameters. For example:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class Tag>
void tagged(auto tag, int... values);
auto* tagged_pair = &tagged<void, char, 2>;
</xmp>

Here the argument order is <code>Tag</code>, the invented type of <code>tag</code>, then the count. The pointer type is <code>void (*)(char, int, int)</code>. The same order applies to a lambda's call operator template.

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll reproduced in R2 and in [[#straw-polls]] records support for the named form. It predates this revision and its count model. [[P1219R2]] [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Check how explicit arguments are assigned when a template parameter pack precedes the count, including constant packs and <code>auto</code> parameters. Keep the count last and test deduction from calls and target types when it cannot be supplied explicitly. We still need to specify these cases.</p>
</aside>

### Type deduction ### {#deduction}

In these proposed declarations, <code>sum</code> fixes the element type as <code>int</code> while <code>total</code> uses the ordinary template parameter <code>T</code>.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

For <code>total</code> without an explicit element type, <code>T</code> is deduced from every applicable argument. This uses the same consistency rule as separate <code>T</code> parameters. It neither chooses the first argument's type nor computes a common type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> With <code>sum</code> or <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>, ordinary conversions initialize the <code>int</code> parameters. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6</a> Supplying only <code>T</code> leaves the count to be deduced. Supplying the count as well fixes the exact length, as in <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code> or <code>total&lt;int, 2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code>.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed calls with a fixed, deduced, or explicitly supplied element type. These calls omit the count argument and deduce it from the call arity.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element type</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Fixed <code>int</code></td><td><code>sum()</code></td><td>Length zero. The initialized addition fold returns zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sum(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Ordinary conversion initializes both <code>int</code> parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=3>Deduced <code>T</code></td><td><code>total(1, 2)</code></td><td>Deduce <code>T</code> as <code>int</code> and length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Reject conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total()</code></td><td>Reject because there is no argument from which to deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Explicit <code>int</code></td><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Use the explicit element type and ordinary conversions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;()</code></td><td>Length zero with a known element type.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

The function template and its template arguments determine the specialization. The invented count is one of those arguments. Different counts identify different specializations. Converting arguments to a fixed element type does not add their original types to the specialization's identity. The calls <code>sum(1, 2)</code>, <code>sum(1.0, 2.0)</code>, and <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2)</code> therefore select the same specialization, <code>sum&lt;2&gt;</code>. Explicit and deduced counts have the same identity after conversion to the count parameter's type.

Function-type equality alone does not identify a specialization: different template arguments can produce the same adjusted function type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.general#3">[temp.deduct.general]/3-4</a>

An explicitly supplied count fixes the pack length. <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> is valid under the proposal. <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2)</code> and <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> are rejected. <code>sum&lt;0&gt;()</code> is valid and <code>sum&lt;0&gt;(1)</code> is rejected. Count arguments satisfy the ordinary constant template argument rules for the invented unsigned type. An empty template-argument list supplies no count, so <code>sum&lt;&gt;()</code> deduces zero while <code>&amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code> still needs a target type. The invented parameter does not make every expression in the function body dependent.

A zero count makes no deductions for template parameters. An element type must still be supplied explicitly, deduced elsewhere, or obtained from a default. Substitution into the element pattern follows the ordinary substitution rules even when the count is zero.

Ordinary reference binding, list-initialization, parameter adjustment, and lifetime rules apply. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> A braced argument initializes its corresponding parameter and retains narrowing checks. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> A temporary bound to a reference parameter persists until completion of the full-expression containing the call. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> Declaring a pack neither extends that lifetime nor imposes a new evaluation order on argument initialization. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6-7</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test deduction and initialization with move-only arguments, forwarding references, overloaded function names, braced arguments, constraints, exception specifications, and constant evaluation.</p>
</aside>

### Target types for initialization ### {#parameter-initialization}

A fixed parameter type supplies a destination for a braced initializer or an overloaded function name. These proposed declarations illustrate both forms:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

void plot(const Point&... points);

using Step = void (*)();
void start();
template <class T> void start(T mode);
void stop();

void run_all(Step... steps);
</xmp>

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Target typing under the proposed parameter-initialization rules.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>plot({0, 0}, {4, 5})</code></td><td>Each argument initializes a temporary <code>Point</code> bound to a const reference parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>plot({2.5, 3})</code></td><td>Rejected because list-initialization would narrow <code>Point::x</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>run_all(start, stop)</code></td><td>The <code>Step</code> target selects <code>void start()</code> from its overload set.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

A deduced pack cannot infer a type from the bare braces or from the <code>start</code> overload set, which contains a function template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#5">[temp.deduct.type]/5.8-5.9</a> Current C++ already provides those targets through <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Point&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Step&gt;</code> parameters. The corresponding calls are <code>plot({&#123;0, 0}, {4, 5}})</code> and <code>run_all({start, stop})</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> Explicit <code>Point</code> objects and a <code>static_cast&lt;Step&gt;(start)</code> also work with deduced packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.over#1">[over.over]/1-3</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test the braced-initializer examples against constrained packs and initializer lists. Cover deduction, empty calls and initializers, designated initializers, and narrowing.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Advise on deduction-guide support, including deduction and return types, before we decide whether to include it.</p>
</aside>

### Address-taking ### {#addresses}

For the <code>sum</code> and <code>total</code> declarations above, taking an address deduces the count from the target function type. The length-two specialization of <code>sum</code> has the ordinary function type <code>int(int, int)</code>.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed address-taking and declaration behavior for <code>sum</code> and <code>total</code>. Each row is independent. The element type and count both need to match the identified function.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Operation</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int (*zero)() = sum</code></td><td>Select length zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*one)(int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code><br><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>The two target types select different specializations of <code>sum&lt;&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because no length is determined.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because specifying <code>T</code> does not supply the length.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;2&gt;</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int, 2&gt;</code></td><td>Select the two-parameter specialization without a target type.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;3&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because the explicitly fixed length disagrees with the target.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(double, double) = sum</code></td><td>Reject the mismatched parameter types. Call conversions do not adapt a function pointer signature.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int...)</code> and the named definition</td><td>Corresponding declarations of the same template, if reclaiming the comma-less unnamed spelling is approved.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int, int)</code></td><td>An ordinary overload, not an implicit specialization declaration.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

The existing explicit-specialization and explicit-instantiation syntax is retained. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#2">[temp.explicit]/2</a> In these proposed declarations, the ordinary parameter list deduces the omitted count argument:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
int sum<>(int value) {
    return value + 10;
}

template int sum<>(int, int);
</xmp>

The equivalent spellings <code>template &lt;&gt; int sum&lt;1&gt;(int value)</code> and <code>template int sum&lt;2&gt;(int, int)</code> explicitly supply those counts. They identify the same specialization and instantiation as the omitted-count forms, rather than additional entities. A count that disagrees with the declared parameter list is rejected. Ordinary target-type matching applies after count deduction or explicit substitution.

The count identifies the same specialization whether the function is called or its address is taken. A runtime variadic interface would need to represent the arguments and count at runtime, without providing <code>sizeof...</code> for return types or constraints.

### Overload ordering ### {#overload-ordering}

The proposed ordering policy retains ordinary conversion ranking, partial ordering, and constraint subsumption. The element type already participates in those comparisons. We leave otherwise unordered candidates ambiguous rather than add a preference based solely on homogeneous syntax. Partial ordering compares template declarations and retains information about which parameters came from packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a> It does not compare only the resulting fixed-arity specializations. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3-6</a>

A call with no arguments for a pack contributes no element-type comparisons to partial ordering. A default template argument can make a candidate viable. It does not turn the candidate's dependent pattern into a fixed type for ordering. A non-template overload wins a tie against a template specialization under the existing rules. A fixed-arity template still participates in template partial ordering.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed overload outcomes under the ordinary-ordering policy. Each overload set is independent.</caption>
<colgroup><col><col style="width: clamp(8em, 25vw, 10em)"><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Overloads</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>void fixed(int... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void fixed(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>fixed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed(1.0, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the deduced-element template by better conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed()</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template because the other cannot deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void packed(T... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class... Ts&gt; void packed(Ts... values);</code></td>
<td><code>packed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Ambiguous.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed(1, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the heterogeneous template because the homogeneous candidate has conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed()</code></td><td>Select the heterogeneous template because the homogeneous candidate cannot deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void empty(int... values);</code><br>
<code>void empty(double... values);</code></td>
<td><code>empty()</code></td><td>Ambiguous. The zero count supplies no element-type comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void pair(int, int);</code><br>
<code>void pair(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>pair(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the non-template overload after tied conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T, T);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>paired(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-arity template under ordinary template partial ordering.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void single(T);</code><br>
<code>void single(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>single(1)</code></td><td>Ambiguous. The fixed-arity pattern and fixed element type do not establish one template as more specialized.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

The count argument is determined before evaluating an associated constraint that uses the pack length. Constraint satisfaction substitutes ordinary template arguments, including the invented count and the arguments of enclosing specializations. No separate count bindings are needed. Rechecking the same atomic constraint with the same template arguments retains the existing consistency requirement. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#3">[temp.constr.atomic]/3</a>

The count value does not add a test for atomic identity or subsumption. Separate predicates such as <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 1</code> and <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 2</code> gain no arithmetic implication rule. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#2">[temp.constr.atomic]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.order#1.2">[temp.constr.order]/1.2</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test the overload outcomes above with the invented count. Check how corresponding packs are matched in atomic and fold-expanded constraints so we can complete the rules.</p>
</aside>

### Constructors ### {#constructors}

Initializer-list constructors have priority under <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.list#1">[over.match.list]/1</a>. If homogeneous packs are permitted in constructors, those constructors would participate as implicit templates in ordinary overload selection. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2.4">[over.match.best.general]/2.4</a>

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>A conditional constructor comparison. The two-element overload wins against the homogeneous template, while list-initialization prefers the initializer-list constructor.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>If homogeneous constructors are supported</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(std::initializer_list<int>);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(const int&... elems);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Changed constructor selection under ordinary overload ranking.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With initializer list</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2, 3};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the homogeneous constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the explicit two-argument constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values = {1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Rejected because the selected two-argument constructor is explicit.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

Replacing the list constructor would change both the selected overload and whether copy-list-initialization succeeds.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Advise on constructor support and check the examples above with the constructor treated as an implicit template. We still need to decide whether to include it.</p>
</aside>

## Lambdas ## {#lambdas}

Under the proposal, the lambda uses the same count model as a named function so its call operator is a template even though its element type is fixed:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](double... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>

The calls <code>product()</code> and <code>product(0.5, 0.8)</code> return <code>1.0</code> and <code>0.4</code>. The call operator template has the invented count parameter, so <code>product.operator()&lt;2&gt;(0.5, 0.8)</code> selects the same specialization as the second call. A current-language lambda with <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... factors</code> accepts the two ordinary calls, but its template arguments are element types rather than the proposed count.

For this captureless lambda, <code>double (*multiply)(double, double) = product</code> deduces count two through the generic-lambda conversion function template. Unary <code>+product</code> supplies no target type or count and is rejected. The ordinary restrictions on capture, explicit object parameters, constraints, and static call operators continue to apply. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.closure#12">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/12-14</a>

An ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack does not by itself make a lambda generic, including when the element type uses pack indexing. The enclosing pack already determines that parameter list so no independent count parameter is needed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.general#6">[expr.prim.lambda.general]/6</a>

## Pack indexing ## {#indexing}

A count constraint and C++26 pack indexing work the same way for a constrained pack and a homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(double... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>

The caller supplies the index and deduction supplies the pack length. For a runtime <code>double reading</code>, <code>select_sample&lt;1&gt;(reading, 0.5, 1.0)</code> selects the second sample. Using index 3 with the same three samples fails the constraint. A <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... samples</code> pack gives the same results for these calls. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

For a type pack <code>Ts</code>, <code>Ts...[1]</code> selects its second type. When the index is itself a pack, as in <code>Ts...[Is]</code>, pack indexing expands only <code>Ts</code>. The index pack <code>Is</code> remains available to an enclosing expansion. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.15">[temp.variadic]/6.15</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2-4</a>

The following is valid C++26. Each lambda has one unnamed parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
void indexed(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
    (..., [](Ts...[Is]) {});
}
</xmp>

The call <code>indexed&lt;int, double, char&gt;(std::index_sequence&lt;2, 0, 2, 1&gt;{})</code> forms four lambdas with parameter types <code>char</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, and <code>double</code>. The index pack can differ in length from <code>Ts</code> and select a type more than once.

Each row below is independent and assumes enclosing declarations of <code>Ts</code> and <code>Is</code>, with valid selected indices.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Indexed lambda parameters: a scalar index permits a new homogeneous count, while an index pack is expanded by the enclosing declaration or fold.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Spelling</th><th scope=col>C++26 interpretation</th><th scope=col>Proposed interpretation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]) {}</code></td><td>One unnamed parameter of the selected type.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[1]) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack remains for the fold.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]) {})</code></td><td>The fold expands <code>Is</code> into single-parameter lambdas.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The parameter declaration has no unexpanded pack.</td><td>A homogeneous pack of the selected type with its own count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[Is]... values) {}</code></td><td>The parameter declaration expands <code>Is</code> into one parameter list.</td><td>Unchanged. No new count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]... values) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The inner declaration already expands <code>Is</code>.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts... values) {}</code></td><td>Ordinary expansion of the enclosing <code>Ts</code>. This alone does not make the lambda generic.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

In the varying-index form, the parameter declaration's second ellipsis already expands <code>Is</code>. This leaves no unexpanded pack for an enclosing fold. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.fold#2">[expr.prim.fold]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: advice. Eczbek: input.</strong> Resolve whether C++26 <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/25</a> treats unnamed <code>Ts...[1]...</code> as C varargs. Check Clang's parsing before we settle the transition.</p>
</aside>

### Homogeneous packs of indexed types ### {#indexed-homogeneous}

A scalar index selects the proposed homogeneous pack's element type before its invented count argument is deduced:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I, class... Ts>
auto selected_counter() {
    return [](Ts...[I]... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}

auto count_doubles = selected_counter<1, int, double, char>();
</xmp>

The call <code>count_doubles(0.5, 1.0)</code> returns 2. The index selects <code>double</code> from three types, but the lambda's homogeneous count comes from its two arguments. Unlike <code>Ts...[Is]... values</code>, this declaration has no index pack for its second ellipsis to expand.

### Array parameters ### {#arrays}

In C++26, the named declaration <code>int... values[Is]</code> expands <code>Is</code> into array bounds. Ordinary pointer adjustment turns parameters with positive extents 1, 2, and 3 into three <code>int*</code> parameters. Zero extents are independently invalid. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a>

The C++26 <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.name#1">[dcl.name]/1</a> grammar has no array-suffix production for an abstract (unnamed) pack declarator. P2662R3 removed that production when introducing pack indexing. [[P2662R3]]

The named array-pack declaration already expands <code>Is</code>. No unexpanded occurrence of that pack remains for an enclosing fold.

The proposal retains the C++26 abstract-declarator grammar. If the committee approves reclamation, an array alias could supply an unnamed homogeneous parameter type with ordinary adjustment. Its array extent remains separate from the homogeneous count.

Parenthesized unnamed pack declarators are tracked by <a href="https://cplusplus.github.io/CWG/issues/1488.html">CWG1488</a>. This paper leaves that grammar change out of scope because the array-alias alternative above needs no new declarator form.

### Empty packs ### {#empty-and-return}

An empty outer expansion over <code>Is</code> need not instantiate an indexed selection. A fixed index must still designate an element when its type is required. Calling the proposed homogeneous lambda with zero arguments does not remove that requirement from its declaration. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2</a>

## Captures ## {#captures}

Constant template parameters supply values inside a lambda without capture. This current-language function returns such a lambda:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <int... values>
auto constant_sum() {
    return [] { return (0 + ... + values); };
}
</xmp>

Calling <code>constant_sum&lt;1, 2, 3&gt;()()</code> returns <code>6</code>. The values belong to the specialization and are available whenever the returned lambda is called. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a>

The proposed function's parameters are <code>int</code> objects with finite lifetimes. For a runtime <code>int reading</code>, it returns a callback that stores copies of its parameter values:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto deferred_sum(int... values) {
    return [=] { return (0 + ... + values); };
}

auto sum_later = deferred_sum(reading, 2, 3);
int reading_sum = sum_later();
</xmp>

For <code>reading == 1</code>, <code>reading_sum</code> is <code>6</code> even though <code>deferred_sum</code> has returned. Replacing <code>&#91;=]</code> with <code>[]</code> makes a nonempty call ill-formed. Reference capture would leave dangling references when <code>sum_later()</code> is called. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a>

A current-language function with <code>std::same_as&lt;int&gt; auto... values</code> can return the same value-capturing callback. Homogeneous packs follow the ordinary capture and lifetime rules, including capture propagation through nested lambdas. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture#14">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]/14</a>

## Alternatives ## {#alternatives}

We defer changes to specialization syntax and declaration-site lengths. The other alternatives below are rejected for this proposal. The examples illustrate the alternatives, not changes proposed by this revision.

### An additional auto marker ### {#additional-auto}

We reject <code>int auto... values</code> because it uses <code>auto</code> as a template marker even though the element type is already supplied. That gives <code>auto</code> a different role from its use as a placeholder. A type name such as <code>int</code> is not a concept constraint. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general">[dcl.spec.auto.general]</a>

Homogeneous template parameter packs already use <code>std::size_t... values</code>. Requiring <code>std::size_t auto... values</code> only for function parameters would give the two homogeneous declarations different spellings. The unambiguous named form <code>T... values</code> supplies both the element type and the pack declaration without another marker.

### Empty template heads ### {#empty-template-heads}

An empty template head could explicitly mark a declaration as a template without naming its invented count parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK, declares a template with a homogeneous parameter pack
template <>
void foo(float... v);
</xmp>

We reject this spelling because <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> already introduces an explicit specialization of an existing template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> The alternative would also use it to declare a new template. The shown form is syntactically unambiguous, but the pack would have to distinguish those roles even though it already establishes template status on its own. We retain the specialization meaning of the prefix.

R2 reports Richard Smith's opposition to this spelling after San Diego in 2018. The 2019 Kona poll is reproduced in [[#straw-polls]]. [[P1219R2]] Changing specialization syntax could remove the overlap, but that is a separate proposal.

### Alternative specialization syntax ### {#alternative-specializations}

We defer changes to explicit-specialization syntax. Homogeneous packs retain the existing specialization syntax, so adopting them need not depend on a broader change to how specializations are declared. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a>

The alternative removes a prefix that makes a specialization resemble a template declaration. In the shown function declaration, the <code nohighlight>template-id</code> and parameter types would still identify the specialization without <code>template &lt;&gt;</code>: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// function template
template <class T, class U>
void f(T t, U u);

// ok: explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
void f<>(int i, double d);

// proposed: explicit specialization
void f<>(int i, double d);

// ok: ordinary function declaration (proposed: no change)
void f(int i, double d);
</xmp>

That change deserves consideration on its own merits, including its treatment of function, variable, and class specializations. R2 records no Kona poll on this option in 2019 and reports interest in a separate paper. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit template markers ### {#marker}

An alternative requires a marker in the template parameter list for a homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <...>
void foo(int... v);

template <class T, ...>
void bar(T... v);
</xmp>

A marker makes template status visible before the parameter list and could explicitly mark the count parameter. It could also provide an opt-in spelling for unnamed packs without reclaiming comma-less C varargs. The corresponding lambda form would be <code>[]&lt;...&gt;(int... values) {}</code>. Making a lambda generic alone does not change indexing or move <code>Is</code> between expansions.

We reject a required marker because the named pack already establishes template status in the function signature. Abbreviated declarations already obtain template status from their parameters. [[P1141R2]] <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=249">[dcl.fct]/21</a> Requiring every homogeneous declaration to carry a marker would add syntax to the named feature for a compatibility benefit needed only by the unnamed extension.

A marker used only for unnamed packs would avoid that cost for named declarations. For this revision, we prefer requiring a pack name to introducing another declaration form solely to omit it. If reclamation is declined, the named feature can proceed on that basis ([[#compatibility]]). R2's 2019 Clang experience did not establish a need for a marker and included no implementation experience from other compilers. [[P1219R2]]

### Bare template introducer ### {#bare-template-introducer}

An alternative considered in R2 omits the angle brackets when no template parameters are written explicitly: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template void foo(int... v);
</xmp>

We reject this spelling because bare <code>template</code> already introduces an explicit instantiation. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#1">[temp.explicit]/1-2</a> The alternative would use the same introducer for creating a new template and requesting an instantiation of an existing one. The shown declaration could be parsed unambiguously because the homogeneous pack distinguishes it. The abbreviated declaration already uses that distinction without assigning another role to the introducer.

### Explicit lengths ### {#explicit-lengths}

We defer declaration-site length parameters. R2 considered the following spelling before pack indexing: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t Len>
void foo(int...[Len] v);
</xmp>

This alternative gives the count a source name and lets the author choose its position in the template parameter list. Those are benefits the invented count does not supply. For the trailing pack in this revision, deduction supplies the count, <code>sizeof...</code> exposes it, and constraints can restrict it. Callers can also supply the count explicitly where its position permits ([[#abbreviated-syntax]]). These operations cover the present use cases without a separate declaration associating a named length with the pack.

A type-pack variant spelled <code>Ts...[Len]</code> conflicts with C++26 indexed type selection: that spelling already selects one type from <code>Ts</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index">[dcl.type.pack.index]</a> An explicit marker does not resolve that conflict by itself. This rules out that spelling, not declaration-site lengths generally.

Named lengths can be considered alongside multiple homogeneous packs and non-trailing homogeneous packs, with rules for associating and deducing their counts. These extensions are deferred. Restoring removed abstract array-pack grammar would also be a separate change.

### Trivial templates ### {#trivial-templates}

We reject extending the empty-template-head alternative to templates with no template parameters and no homogeneous pack. A homogeneous function template already has the invented count parameter, even when its element type is fixed. Allowing an empty source <code nohighlight>template-head</code> would not remove that parameter, so parameterless templates are not needed for this feature.

The further extension would also make the same declaration serve as either a trivial-template redeclaration or an explicit specialization:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// #1: trivial function template
template <>
void f(int v);

// #2: function template with homogeneous pack
template <>
void f(int... vs);

// #3: redeclaration of #1 or explicit specialization of #2?
template <>
void f(int v);
</xmp>

A trivial function template could be defined in a header without <code>inline</code> and the extension would make <code>[]&lt;&gt;(){}</code> well-formed. Those conveniences do not justify introducing the specialization ambiguity shown here into the homogeneous-pack proposal.

# Reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling # {#compatibility}

We propose C++29 for both homogeneous packs and removal of deprecated comma-less C varargs. The named feature and reclamation have separate proposed EWG polls ([[#proposed-polls]]). Unnamed homogeneous packs require reclaiming the conflicting spelling. If reclamation is declined, the named feature can proceed with the existing unnamed disambiguation rule retained. A separate removal paper remains a fallback if EWG requests it.

Jan Schultke's [[P3176R1]], <em>The Oxford variadic comma</em>, proposed deprecation rather than removal. N5050 contains the C++26 deprecation while preserving the old meaning and standalone ellipsis parameters. [[N5050]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a> Reclamation would make <code>void send(int... values)</code> and <code>void send(int...)</code> declarations of the same template. C varargs would retain <code>void send(int, ...)</code>.

C++29 gives one standard cycle after C++26 deprecation. That interval is the proposed migration period, not evidence that deployed code will have migrated. Inserting a comma preserves the old meaning in earlier language modes, which permits migration before adopting C++29. The old survey below does not establish current prevalence, warning deployment, or migration cost for maintained and vendored code. EWG needs to weigh those gaps against enabling optional pack names now.

The comma-subscript transition supplies a precedent for one cycle: [[P1161R3]] deprecated unparenthesized comma subscripts for C++20 and [[P2128R6]] proposed the C++23 replacement. P2128R6 also deliberately withheld new multidimensional meaning from C arrays and existing <code>valarray</code> interfaces. It therefore does not establish that immediate reinterpretation of comma-less varargs is harmless. A longer migration period remains a design choice if EWG finds one cycle insufficient.

Requiring pack names avoids reclamation but adds names to declarations that do not otherwise need them. An explicit marker ([[#marker]]) could preserve optional names with extra syntax. These alternatives do not require delaying the named feature.

## Current rules and history ## {#current-comma-rules}

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll in [[#straw-polls]] supported making the varargs comma mandatory. [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

A <em>varargs ellipsis</em> is a C-style ellipsis rather than a parameter-pack ellipsis. C requires an intervening comma when a parameter list precedes it. C23 also permits an ellipsis alone: [[N3096]]

<pre nohighlight no-line-numbers>
<code nohighlight>parameter-type-list:</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
</pre>

C++26 also permits a standalone varargs ellipsis. Unlike C, it permits a varargs ellipsis after a parameter list without a comma. The comma-less alternative is deprecated in C++26: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

<pre nohighlight no-line-numbers>
<code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-clause:</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list</code><sub>opt</sub>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list ...</code>
</pre>

C++ introduced varargs ellipses with function prototypes and initially prohibited a comma before the ellipsis. C later adopted both features but required a comma to distinguish the last formal parameter from varargs. C++ then made the comma optional for C compatibility. [[P1219R2]]

When an ellipsis at the end of a function parameter list could be part of an abstract (nameless) declarator, the parameter's type determines its meaning. If the type names an unexpanded template parameter pack or contains <code>auto</code>, the ellipsis declares a pack. Otherwise, it is a varargs ellipsis. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2026/n5050.pdf#page=250">[dcl.fct]/25</a>

For example, these declarations have different meanings in C++26:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// declares a variadic function template with a function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T...);

// same as void f(T, ...)
template <class T>
void f(T...);
</xmp>

## Proposed change ## {#proposed-comma-change}

Reclamation requires a comma between a parameter list and a C-varargs ellipsis. It removes the old unnamed disambiguation rule and permits unnamed homogeneous packs. The named feature alone does neither. Standalone <code>...</code>, comma-separated C varargs, and ordinary parameter-pack expansions keep their meanings.

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data>
<caption>Comma-less declarations before and after the proposed C++29 reclamation. Each declaration is independent. In the last row, <code>Ts</code> is an existing enclosing template type parameter pack.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>C++26 declaration</th><th scope=col>After reclamation</th><th scope=col>Migration preserving C varargs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>void f(int value...)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The name precedes the ellipsis</td><td><code>void f(int value, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(int...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with fixed element type int</td><td><code>void f(int, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with element type T</td><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(Ts......)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The final C ellipsis lacks a comma</td><td><code>void f(Ts..., ...)</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

For the six-dot row, <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; struct S { static void f(Ts......); };</code> is a valid existing declaration. <code>S&lt;int&gt;::f(1, 2.0)</code> passes its second argument through C varargs. The migration inserts a comma between the expansion ellipsis and the C ellipsis. Named expansions similarly change <code>Ts... values...</code> to <code>Ts... values, ...</code>. Removing the last ellipsis instead would change the interface. [[P3176R1]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a>

The first and last rows become ill-formed and require a diagnostic. The middle rows remain valid with new meanings, so no diagnostic follows merely from reclamation. For example, an old <code>f(1, 2.5)</code> call to <code>void f(int...)</code> passed a double through C varargs. Its new meaning initializes two int parameters, including an ordinary floating-integral conversion. Empty calls become possible. The specializations have fixed parameter counts and no C ellipsis. This is a source and potential link compatibility break under [[SD10]], even when recompilation succeeds.

### Migrating definitions ### {#migrating-definitions}

Definitions need migration along with declarations. A legacy definition using <code>va_start</code> still needs an actual C ellipsis. The homogeneous declaration supplies ordinary parameters and cannot preserve that definition by itself. In C++26 the second and subsequent macro arguments of <code>va_start</code> are discarded, so a surviving parameter name is not a portable diagnostic mechanism. Implementations can reject a builtin invocation in a non-variadic specialization, but the C-library preconditions are not a promise of a required diagnostic. Add the comma to retain the original C-varargs definition. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/cstdarg.syn">[cstdarg.syn]</a>

A declaration and definition migrated inconsistently can introduce a homogeneous template and a separate C-varargs overload instead of a matching pair. That can be valid at the declaration level. Calls can become ambiguous or select the template, whose missing definition can surface during instantiation or linking. The observed compile or link failure depends on the program, available definitions, and implementation. Changing the declaration does not provide ABI compatibility with an existing C-varargs definition.

### Function types and overload resolution ### {#function-types-and-overload-resolution}

Function-pointer types also need the comma: <code>using F = void(int...)</code> becomes ill-formed because a function type alias cannot introduce a homogeneous function template. Its migration is <code>using F = void(int, ...)</code>. An old C-varargs pointer target no longer matches a reclaimed homogeneous declaration. A fixed-arity pointer target can instead select a specialization with a deduced count ([[#addresses]]).

Overload resolution can change because ordinary parameter conversions replace ellipsis conversion sequences. Metaprogramming that probes callability, takes addresses, or recognizes C-varargs function types can therefore produce different answers while remaining well-formed. Preserve the comma throughout declarations, definitions, pointer and member-pointer types, traits, and macro-generated declarations when the intent is C varargs. An ordinary expansion such as <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; void f(Ts...)</code> is unchanged.

[[P3776R1]], <em>More trailing commas</em>, proposes trailing commas in function parameter lists but excludes them after C ellipsis parameters. Its grammar needs coordination with reclamation, including the comma between an ordinary pack and C varargs.

## The impact of a breaking change ## {#compatibility-survey}

The survey and Boost analysis below come from P1219R2 (2019) and do not survey current code. R2 describes the ACTCD19 dataset at codesearch.isocpp.org (2019 archive). It reports 876,800,403 lines of source code across 2,489,599 files from open-source projects, citing the codesearch FAQ. The survey used this dataset to estimate the impact of requiring a comma before a varargs ellipsis. [[P1219R2]] [[CODESEARCH-FAQ]]

<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data numeric-table">
<caption>Comma-less varargs survey reported in P1219R2, 2019. [[P1219R2]]</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Classification</th><th scope=col>Lines</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Lines containing ellipses</td><td>258,196</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, with comma</td><td>95,724</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, without comma</td><td>6,318</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost</td><td>6,182</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost, deduplicated</td><td>879</td></tr>
<tr><td>All others</td><td>136</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

R2 reports seventeen complete or partial Boost copies in the dataset. It attributes 416 of the 879 unique Boost hits to a member-function-pointer traits header covering arities from 0 to 24, qualifiers, calling conventions, and varargs. That is evidence of metaprogramming impact in the 2019 corpus, not evidence that those declarations have since been migrated. The header-only search, textual filtering, retained inactive code, and unexpanded macros limit what can be inferred about compiled uses or current migration cost. [[P1219R2]]

The survey used regular expression searches and human review because a C++ parser with sufficient language support and build scripts for each project were unavailable.

The survey considered only headers with extensions <code>.h</code>, <code>.hh</code>, <code>.hpp</code>, or <code>.hxx</code>. It stripped preprocessor directives, then ran each file through the C++ preprocessor to remove comments. Because the macro definitions had been removed, invocations remained unexpanded. Inactive blocks, including <code>#if 0</code>, also remained. Scanning the output for ellipses yielded 258,196 lines, each tagged with its source file.

Lines with identifiers, operators, or other patterns characteristic of pack declarations or expansions were excluded from further analysis.

In 89,614 lines, a comma immediately preceded an ellipsis. These lines unambiguously declared varargs functions.

Of 8,101 lines beginning with an ellipsis, 6,241 appeared to come from function declarations. Of those 6,241 lines, 6,092 had a comma immediately before the ellipsis on a preceding line. Of the remaining 149 lines, 131 were catch clauses or declarations with only an ellipsis. The last 18 mixed static-analysis macros into parameter lists. None of these ellipses is ambiguous with a pack declaration.

James Touton's [[P0281R0]] proposed removal in 2016 and recognized that existing code would break. P3176R1 later attributed that proposal's failure to compatibility impact and chose deprecation without a replacement feature. Those accounts establish a compatibility concern, not the reasons for each Belfast vote on P1219. The public Belfast result below records failure without an explanation from the opposing voters. [[P3176R1]] [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

