The explicit template argument determines the element type but leaves the count argument undetermined. The proposed <code>auto p = &amp;f&lt;int, 3&gt;</code> would instead identify <code>void(int, int, int)</code> without a target type.

### Function calls ### {#wording-call-deduction}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>, make the inline change and insert before Example 2:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>For a function parameter pack that occurs at the end of the <span class=grammar>parameter-declaration-list</span>, deduction is performed for each remaining argument of the call, taking the type <em>P</em> of the <span class=grammar>declarator-id</span> of the function parameter pack as the corresponding function template parameter type. Each deduction deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack. When a function parameter pack appears in a non-deduced context (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type">[temp.deduct.type]</a>), the type of that pack is never deduced.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack whose count argument has not been explicitly specified, deduce that argument as the number of call arguments remaining after those associated with the preceding parameters. If the count argument has been explicitly specified, the number of remaining call arguments shall equal it. Deduction from the parameter pattern is performed independently for each corresponding argument for template parameters whose arguments have not been explicitly specified. Deductions for the same template parameter, including those from preceding function parameters, are combined under the ordinary deduction rules. If no call arguments remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>After deduction and substitution, each argument initializes its corresponding function parameter under the ordinary initialization rules. An explicitly supplied element type is not deduced again. An argument-count mismatch or inconsistent deduction causes template argument deduction to fail.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test deduction with preceding default arguments, explicit object parameters, and pack-expansion arguments. Include empty packs and conversions to fixed or explicitly supplied element types.</p>
</aside>

Retain the existing <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a> example unchanged.

### Function types ### {#wording-type-deduction}

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#10">[temp.deduct.type]/10</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>When a function parameter list containing a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack is matched against a function parameter list of known length, deduce an omitted argument for the count parameter as the number of parameters remaining after matching the preceding parameters. An explicitly supplied count shall equal that number. Match the parameter pattern against each remaining parameter using the applicable deduction rules. If no parameters remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack. This deduction applies when identifying a specialization from a target function type or from an explicit specialization or explicit instantiation declaration. It does not apply call-argument conversions. In the absence of an explicit count and a function type from which to deduce it, the count remains undetermined and no specialization is identified.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test matching against target function types, explicit specializations, and instantiations, including parameter type adjustments. Reject undetermined counts and type mismatches that only a call-site conversion could accept.</p>
</aside>

### Partial ordering and constraints ### {#wording-partial-ordering}

This candidate wording specifies the policy in [[#overload-ordering]].

Insert after <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3</a>:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>To produce the transformed template, for each type, constant, type template, variable template, or concept template parameter (including template parameter packs (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>) thereof) synthesize a unique type, value, class template, variable template, or concept, respectively, and substitute it for each occurrence of that parameter in the function type of the template. The type replacing a placeholder in the type of the value synthesized for a constant template parameter is a unique synthesized type.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>The transformation preserves each homogeneous function parameter pack and applies the usual transformation to all template parameters, including its count parameter. The parameter pattern is not expanded to the count deduced for a candidate specialization.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

In <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For partial ordering, the pattern of a homogeneous function parameter pack is its parameter pattern (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>). Deduction against this pattern combines deductions for template parameters under the usual consistency requirements. Comparison of two pack patterns does not determine their count arguments from the arity of a candidate specialization. A parameter transformed from a homogeneous function parameter pack remains a parameter transformed from a function parameter pack for the purposes of this subclause.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

The existing tie-breaks apply without an additional preference for a homogeneous pack.

## Constraint satisfaction ## {#wording-constraint-satisfaction}

Retain <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#1">[temp.constr.atomic]/1-3</a> unchanged. The invented count argument is substituted with the other template arguments, including within enclosing specializations. Separate count bindings and additional constraint-satisfaction inputs are unnecessary. The existing consistency rule therefore covers explicit and deduced count arguments.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test pack-length and fold-expanded constraints across matching declarations. Check that matching declarations preserve the correspondence between packs and their count parameters. Check that count values add no new constraint-identity or subsumption rule.</p>
</aside>

## Declarations ## {#wording-correspondence}

The additional correspondence rule associates the count parameter with the homogeneous function pack in each declaration. Corresponding homogeneous packs have corresponding parameter patterns and invented count parameters in the same template parameter position. Parameter names do not distinguish declarations. An ordinary expansion of an existing pack does not correspond to a newly declared homogeneous pack merely because a specialization produces the same function type.

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: review. Eczbek: implementation.</strong> Test declaration and specialization identity across translation units, modules, and serialized ASTs. Check definition visibility and explicit instantiations separately from ABI representation.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Ian: wording. Jan: advice. Eczbek: input.</strong> Confirm whether the PoC can test reflection. If supported, check that it exposes the count in template-argument order, identifies the function template, and reports the resulting parameters, including none at count zero.</p>
</aside>

## Feature-test macro ## {#wording-feature-test}

<aside class=issue>
<p><strong>Everyone: decision. Eczbek: input.</strong> Advise on a feature-test macro for the implementation. The macro and revision value still need agreement with SG10 once the feature scope is settled.</p>
</aside>

# Conclusion # {#conclusion}

We seek agreement on one trailing homogeneous function parameter pack for C++29, with ordinary parameter initialization and an invented constant template parameter for its length. Calls and target function types can deduce that argument or the programmer can supply it explicitly. The resulting specializations have ordinary fixed-arity function types. Declaration-site length syntax and multiple homogeneous packs remain out of scope.

We also seek a separate C++29 decision to remove deprecated comma-less C varargs and reclaim the spelling for unnamed homogeneous packs. Those unnamed packs depend on reclamation. The named feature, including explicit count arguments, can proceed independently. A separate removal paper remains a fallback if EWG feedback warrants it. The migration analysis identifies meaning changes and the limits of the available usage evidence. EWG therefore still needs to decide whether one cycle is sufficient.

